seg-787705fa0fac = A Lunar Coordinate Time scale (LTC/TCL (TCL: Temps-Coordonnée Lunaire, the French name of Lunar Coordinate Time)) requires the secular rate at which a lunar-surface clock runs relative to an Earth-geoid (TT) clock. <c1/> computes it from first principles: a first post-Newtonian identity summing the <b>self-potential difference</b> (the IAU <c2/> conventional geoid potential <c3/> minus the Moon's surface self-potential <c4/>) and the <b>kinetic (second-order Doppler) term</b> <c5/> from the geocentric Moon velocity (finite-differenced from the Montenbruck-Gill lunar series in <c6/>). The dominant self-potential term gives <b>≈ { "57.5" } µs/day</b>, the kinetic term <b>≈ −{ "0.5" } µs/day</b>, for a total <b>≈ { "57" } µs/day</b>. The <c7/> scenario reports this rate, the accumulated LTC−TT offset over a horizon, and supports a minimal inverse-variance ensemble (a lunar paper-clock); TT↔LTC conversions round-trip to < { "1" } ns/day.

